 "amendments": {
  "items": {
   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the agriculture and markets law, in relation to labeling requirements for fish wholesalers",
    "basePrintNo": "S1422",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S1422-2017",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Add §214-o, amd §190, Ag & Mkts L",
    "lawSection": "Agriculture and Markets Law",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S1422\n\n TITLE OF BILL :\n\nAn act to amend the agriculture and markets law, in relation to\nlabeling requirements for fish wholesalers\n\n PURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL :\n\nThis legislation would amend the Agriculture and markets law to\nrequire that wholesalers of fish may only sell such fish that is in\nsealed and clearly labeled containers; and to require that such labels\ninclude: 1) the identity and weight and standard measure or numerical\ncount of the commodity, 2) the name and address of the shipper,\npacker, or distributor, 3) the state of commodity origin.\n\n SUMMARY OF PROVISIONS :\n\nSection 1: Amends Agriculture and Markets Law section 190(2) to\nprovide the proper cross reference to the new bill section 2 of this\nact that creates Agriculture and Markets Law 214-o.\n\nSection 2: Adds a new Ag. & Markets Law section 214-o, which would\nrequire that any wholesaler of fish may only sell fish for human\nconsumption if it is sealed in clearly labeled containers. The labels\nmust include the following: 1) the identity and weight and standard\nmeasure or numerical count of the commodity, 2) the name and address\nof the shipper, packer, or distributor, 3) the state of commodity\norigin.\n\nIn addition, this bill adds a provision so that these new state\nprovisions are to be deemed to comply with federal law and regulations\nso that no new duplicative standards need to be implements. In\nessence, this bill allows New York State to enforce the federal and\nstate labeling requirements so that it is clear the kinds of fish that\nis being sold and what is the weight of such fish.\n\nSection 3: Effective 180 days after it shall have become law\n\n JUSTIFICATION :\n\nUnder the existing federal law, wholesalers of fish are required to\nattach labels that provide correct information of the weight, identity\nand origin of the fish offered for sale. In addition, federal law\nrequires the label to contain the name and address of the shipper,\npacker, or distributor of the fish product.\n\nWhile law exists to prevent mislabeling of fish, media reports and\nstudies indicate that there is widespread non-compliance with existing\nfederal statutes in labeling fish products. Various incidents have\nbeen reported at wholesale markets where wholesalers remove the\noriginal label affixed on fish containers only to replace them with a\nnew label or handwritten note that provides inaccurate counts and net\nweight (short-weight), and misidentifies the name and origin of fish.\nThese instances result in retailers receiving less for their money,\ncosting legitimate business sales, and reducing confidence in the fish\nproduct purchased by both retailers and consumers.\n\nThis bill proposes to address the problem of weight deception, species\nsubstitution and improper labeling by enacting an explicit requirement\nthat fish must be accurately labeled at the wholesale level. In\naddition, this bill was amended to be deemed to comply with federal\nlaw and regulations with regard to the wholesale labeling of fish, so\nthat no additional or duplicative standards for labeling are needed.\n\n\nThe labeling standards that are being established by this bill for\nfish are the same state standards that are currently used for the\nwholesale labeling of other agricultural products such as apples,\npears, other fruits, vegetables, and other agricultural produce.\n\n LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :\n\n2016: S.1147-C/A.7461-B (Kim)-Passed Senate\n2015: S.1147-B/A.7461 (Kim)\n2014: S.7730\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATIONS :\n\nNone.\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nThis bill takes effect 180 days after it shall have become law;\nprovided, however that effective immediately, the addition, amendment\nand/or repeal of any rule or regulation necessary for the\nimplementation of this act on its effective date are authorized and\ndirected to be made and completed on or before such effective date.\n",
